  "worked_solution": "We differentiate $v(t)$ to calculate $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{ln}\u2061t+4t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061t+1+4 \\\\  & =5+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the time when the acceleration is $a=6,$ we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =5+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061t & =1 \\\\ {e}^{(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061t)} & ={e}^{1} \\\\ t & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, to calculate the velocity at time $t=e,$ we substitute this into the equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(e) & =(e\\operatorname{ln}\u2061e+4e) \\\\  & =e(1)+4e \\\\  & =5e\\text{\u00a0m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity when $a=6\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ is $5e\\text{\u00a0m/s}.$",
